Mark SACLIB's optional sacMain() hook as a weak reference.

src/main.c defines the strong symbol main(), whose whole purpose is to call the
optional hook sacMain() so that a program can provide "int sacMain(int,char**)"

- either its own main() or SACLIB's.  No other translation unit in the library
  references main() or sacMain(), so the archive member is normally not pulled
  in; but as soon as a consumer does reference main() (for example a program
  that links the archive with --whole-archive, or that uses SACLIB's own main()
  while defining sacMain() itself), the strong definition of main() clashes
  with the consumer's main() and the strong undefined sacMain() breaks programs
  that do not have the hook.

Declaring the hook weak fixes both directions and is the same treatment the
Debian/Fedora saclib package applies.
diff --git a/include/sacsys.h b/include/sacsys.h
index 0000000..3944ba9 100644
--- a/include/sacsys.h
+++ b/include/sacsys.h
@@ -52,6 +52,16 @@
 # endif
 #endif
 
+/* Indicate that a symbol is a weak reference.  SACLIB calls the optional
+ * sacMain() hook from its default main(); the hook is declared weak so that a
+ * program which does not provide it still links. */
+#ifndef __weak
+# ifdef __GNUC__
+#  define __weak __attribute__ ((weak))
+# else
+#  define __weak
+# endif
+#endif
 
 
 #endif
diff --git a/src/main.c b/src/main.c
index 0000000..eb49aa7 100644
--- a/src/main.c
+++ b/src/main.c
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 ======================================================================*/
 #include "saclib.h"
 
-extern int sacMain  P__((int ac, char **av));
+extern int sacMain  P__((int ac, char **av)) __weak;
 
 
 void main(argc, argv)
